# Nim conventions

## Style
- 2-space indentation, NEP-1 naming (`camelCase` procs/vars, `PascalCase` types).
- Prefer `std/[a, b]` grouped imports. Import only what the module uses; do not
  re-export stdlib modules except deliberately from `src/neel.nim`.
- Every public symbol gets a `##` doc comment. Every module starts with a `##`
  header stating its single responsibility; update the header when the module's
  surface or contract changes.
- Exceptions are `object of CatchableError` named `Neel<Thing>Error`
  (`NeelTimeoutError`, `NeelNoWindowError`, `NeelServeModeError`).
- Match the surrounding code's comment density. Comments state constraints the
  code cannot show; they never cite `SPEC.md`, step numbers, or the log.

## Deprecated / forbidden stdlib
- `std/threadpool` and `spawn`: use `std/typedthreads` + `std/locks` and the pool
  in `src/neel/pool.nim`.
- `std/sha1`: use `src/neel/sha1.nim`.
- `std/asyncdispatch`: Neel is threaded, not async.

## Threading
- Code that runs on worker or IO threads must be `{.gcsafe.}`. Shared state is
  either immutable after init, protected by a `Lock`, or passed through a
  bounded queue. Use `{.cast(gcsafe).}` only on write-once-at-init data and say
  why in a comment.
- Never block the IO thread (`server.nim` selectors loop). Anything that can
  wait, parse large input, or call user code goes to the pool.
- Every blocking wait has a deadline. No unbounded `sleep` loops for lifecycle
  logic.
- Free memory while the thread that grew it is alive: Nim's allocator cannot
  take a block back after its owning thread exits. Follow the existing pattern
  (the IO thread clears its own tables before parking; shared state is freed in
  `shutdown`'s `beforeJoin`).
- Hooks (`onWindowOpen`, `onWindowClose`) never run while the window lock is
  held: collect under the lock, release, then call.
- Signal handlers (POSIX `sigaction`, Windows `SetConsoleCtrlHandler`) only set
  an atomic flag or call `_exit`. No locks, allocation, condition variables, or
  I/O in a handler; `quitApp()` is not safe there.

## Platform code
- Platform branches use `when defined(windows)` / `when defined(posix)` (see
  `server.nim` and `browser.nim` for the existing patterns). Code that only one
  OS can exercise needs a manual check listed in the step's notes.

## Macros
- `expose` and `startApp` are macros; keep generated code small and delegate
  real work to ordinary procs so it is testable and error messages point at
  user code.
- `startApp` forwards unknown keyword arguments verbatim to `runApp`; new
  `startApp` parameters are added to `runApp`'s signature, not the macro.
- Use `error(msg, node)` with the user's node for compile-time diagnostics.
- When a macro must reference a symbol from another module, splice the bound
  `nnkSym` (`bindSym`), never a bare ident.

## Example

```nim
## websocket.nim - RFC 6455 frame encoding and decoding. No socket IO here.

type
  NeelFrameError* = object of CatchableError
    ## Raised on a malformed or oversized frame.

proc decodeFrame*(buf: openArray[byte]; maxLen: int): Frame {.gcsafe.} =
  ## Decodes one frame from `buf`; raises `NeelFrameError` if invalid.
```
